# Real Hyperledger Fabric setup

This guide converts the application from a UI prototype into a real permissioned-ledger deployment. The backend should use `FABRIC_MODE=fabric` and a client identity that belongs to the organization whose transactions it submits.

## 1. Prerequisites
- Windows 10/11 with Docker Desktop.
- WSL2 enabled and Docker Desktop WSL integration enabled.
- Node.js 24 LTS in the Windows development environment.
- A Hyperledger Fabric 2.x network with peer gateway access.
- Four logical organizations in the project model: Manufacturer, Distributor, Pharmacy and Regulator.

## 2. Fabric network
Use the official Hyperledger Fabric samples as the local network foundation. Create a channel named `pharmachannel` (or change the application configuration to match your network).

Example from the Fabric `test-network` directory in WSL:
```bash
./network.sh down
./network.sh up createChannel -c pharmachannel
```

Deploy this repository's JavaScript chaincode using the chaincode deployment process for the Fabric samples release you selected. Record the exact Fabric samples release/commit in the final report.

## 3. Backend configuration
In `backend/.env`:
```env
PORT=4000
JWT_SECRET=<strong-random-secret>
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=8h
FABRIC_MODE=fabric
USE_POSTGRES=true
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://pharma:pharma@localhost:5432/pharma_traceability
IPFS_API_URL=http://localhost:5001/api/v0
FABRIC_MSP_ID=ManufacturerMSP
FABRIC_CHANNEL=pharmachannel
FABRIC_CHAINCODE=pharmatrace
FABRIC_GATEWAY_ENDPOINT=localhost:7051
FABRIC_TLS_CERT_PATH=C:\path\to\peer\tls\ca.crt
FABRIC_CERT_PATH=C:\path\to\user\signcerts\cert.pem
FABRIC_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=C:\path\to\user\keystore\priv_sk
```

The certificate, key and TLS paths must match the actual organization identity used by the API process. For a multi-organization production deployment, issue and manage distinct identities instead of sharing one backend identity.

## 4. PostgreSQL
From PowerShell:
```powershell
cd "C:\Users\N I T R O 5\Downloads\pharma-blockchain-traceability-capstone-complete\pharma-blockchain-traceability-capstone-complete"
docker compose up -d postgres
cd ".\backend"
npm install
npm run db:init
```

## 5. IPFS
```powershell
cd "C:\Users\N I T R O 5\Downloads\pharma-blockchain-traceability-capstone-complete\pharma-blockchain-traceability-capstone-complete"
docker compose up -d ipfs
```

The application stores a SHA-256 hash on the batch record and can use IPFS for document persistence. Treat the CID as a content address, while the hash is the integrity value used for verification experiments.

## 6. Start the real application
Backend:
```powershell
cd "C:\Users\N I T R O 5\Downloads\pharma-blockchain-traceability-capstone-complete\pharma-blockchain-traceability-capstone-complete\backend"
npm test
npm run dev
```

Frontend:
```powershell
cd "C:\Users\N I T R O 5\Downloads\pharma-blockchain-traceability-capstone-complete\pharma-blockchain-traceability-capstone-complete\frontend"
npm run dev
```

## 7. Validate real ledger evidence
Capture:
- channel creation output;
- chaincode deployment output;
- successful Product and Batch transaction IDs;
- Transfer/Receive/Verify/Dispense transaction IDs;
- query results from the ledger;
- block/transaction evidence where available;
- application audit records in PostgreSQL;
- document CID and SHA-256 evidence when documents are used.

Only publish measured Fabric latency/TPS results in the thesis after the real network experiment has been executed.
